content.text:
The two SDKs do the same job. A caller builds a create request, the client checks it, the provider turns the fields it understands into a Daytona or Modal call, and anything left over is rejected before that call. The examples below are the same operations as they exist today.

## Creating a sandbox

Both examples start a Daytona sandbox from the snapshot `daytona-small`.

TypeScript is a plain object. `timeout: 120_000` means 120 seconds. `source` holds exactly one kind, here `snapshot`:

```4:12:examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-from-snapshot/src/main.ts
await using client = new Client({
  provider: daytona(),
  auth: { apiKey: { key: process.env.DAYTONA_API_KEY! } },
  timeout: 120_000,
  // ...
});

const sandbox = await client.create({ source: { snapshot: {  reference: "daytona-small"  } } });
```

Go builds the protobuf message directly. A timeout is a `durationpb` via `sandbox.Duration`. An optional string is a wrapper via `sandbox.Value`. The snapshot is three nested messages because protobuf models "exactly one source" as a oneof named `Kind`:

```20:43:examples/go/daytona/create-daytona-from-snapshot/main.go
	config := &sandbox.Config{
		Auth: &sandbox.AuthConfig{
			Credentials: &sandbox.APIKey{
				ApiKey: &sandbox.APIKeyCredentials{
					Key: os.Getenv("DAYTONA_API_KEY"),
				},
			},
		},
		Timeout: sandbox.Duration(2 * time.Minute),
	}
	// ...
	instance, err := client.Create(context.Background(), &sandbox.CreateOptions{
		Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Snapshot{Snapshot: &sandbox.SnapshotSource{Reference: "daytona-small"}}},
	})
```

Inside `Create`, both languages then do the same three steps: validate the request, ask the Daytona mapper to copy the fields it supports, and reject whatever is still set.

## What the mapper does with a field it does not support

Suppose the TypeScript request also sets placement:

```ts
await client.create({
  source: { snapshot: { reference: "daytona-small" } },
  placement: { region: "us" },
});
```

Daytona's mapper sees `placement` and stops before calling Daytona:

```85:89:sdks/typescript/providers/daytona/src/mappings/mappings.ts
  if (request.placement !== undefined)
    unsupported(
      CreateFieldPlacement,
      "per-request Daytona placement is unsupported; use Config.region",
    );
```

The error says the field is `placement` and the kind is unsupported. The Go provider reaches the same decision by a different route. It copies each section it understands onto a Daytona struct and clears that section on a leftover copy. At the end, `RejectUnmapped` walks every field still set on that leftover:

```55:57:sdks/go/providers/daytona/creation.go
	if err := sdk.RejectUnmapped("daytona", planned.rest); err != nil {
		return createPlan{}, err
	}
```

If `Placement` is still set, the error's `Field` is the protobuf name `placement`, while the message lists the Go struct name `Placement`. That walk also skips protobuf bookkeeping fields by their Go names (`state`, `sizeCache`, `unknownFields`). The same helper is what makes `providerOptions` fail in Go: nothing copies it, so it is still set when the walk runs. TypeScript checks it by name:

```ts
if (request.providerOptions !== undefined)
  unsupported("providerOptions", "Daytona provider options are not mapped");
```

So this request never reaches Daytona or Modal in either language:

```ts
await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } },
  providerOptions: { gpu: "A10" },
});
```

`providerOptions` is on the public request type, and every provider rejects it.

## Why an empty list has to survive a copy

An omitted allowlist means "leave the provider default." An empty one means "deny all." Those are different.

```ts
network: { outboundDomains: [] }   // caller set deny-all
network: {}                        // caller said nothing about domains
```

Go copies the request with `proto.Clone` before clearing mapped fields. `proto.Clone` turns an empty slice into a nil slice, which is the same as "not set." Each provider then walks the copy with reflection and puts the empty slice back, so the deny-all check still sees it:

```9:18:sdks/go/providers/daytona/clone_message.go
// cloneMessage copies a protobuf message without copying its embedded mutex.
// proto.Clone drops empty slices and maps; those are restored so an explicit
// empty collection still counts as set.
func cloneMessage[T proto.Message](src T) T {
	// ...
	dst := proto.Clone(src).(T)
	preserveEmpty(value, reflect.ValueOf(dst))
```

Daytona and Modal each have their own copy of that function. After the empty list is preserved, Daytona still rejects it (`empty domain allowlist mapping is not verified`). Modal rejects an empty inbound list for the same reason: the native SDK cannot represent deny-all. The copy step exists so that rejection happens because the list was empty, rather than because the clone forgot the list was there.

## Stopping a sandbox

Both examples load a sandbox, check that this provider implements stop, then stop it.

TypeScript `stop` returns the metadata the provider just sent back, and the log prints that value:

```25:28:examples/typescript/daytona/stop-daytona-sandbox/src/main.ts
const sandbox = await client.get(process.env.DAYTONA_SANDBOX_ID!);
assertCapability(sandbox, LifecycleCapability.Stop);
const info = await sandbox.stop();
console.log(`Daytona sandbox stopped: ${sandbox.id} (${info.state})`);
```

Go `Stop` receives the same metadata, stores it on the handle, and returns only an error. The state is read with a second call:

```45:51:examples/go/daytona/stop-daytona-sandbox/main.go
	if !instance.Supports(sandbox.LifecycleCapabilityStop) {
		log.Fatalf("sandbox %s does not support stop", instance.ID())
	}
	if err := instance.Stop(context.Background(), nil); err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	fmt.Printf("Daytona sandbox stopped: %s (%s)\n", instance.ID(), instance.Info().State)
```

`Supports` answers "does this provider implement stop?" Daytona does, so the call continues. Modal does not, so `Stop` returns `ErrorKindUnsupported` and Modal is never called. Daytona can still reject that particular sandbox after the call goes out. That rejection comes back as Daytona's error.

`Config.region` is the default for every sandbox that client creates. `CreateOptions.placement` is the location for one sandbox. They exist because Daytona and Modal put location in different places.

Daytona’s client has a `Target`. Kit copies `Config.region` there once, when the client is opened:

```231:234:sdks/go/providers/daytona/provider_mappings.go
	if source.Region != nil {
		value := source.GetRegion()
		target.Target = value
	}
```

```go
client, err := sandbox.NewClient(daytona.New(), &sandbox.Config{
    Region: sandbox.Value("us"),
})
instance, err := client.Create(ctx, nil)
```

That `"us"` applies to this create and to the next one. Daytona’s own create params also have a per-call `Target`, and Kit does not map it. A `placement` on the create request is leftover after mapping, so Kit rejects it and Daytona is not called.

Modal’s client has no region. Location is `Cloud` and `Regions` on each `sandboxes.create`. Kit still accepts `Config.region` and uses it as the default list for every create that does not set its own regions:

```86:88:sdks/go/providers/modal/creation.go
	if region != "" {
		c.plan.params.Regions = []string{region}
	}
```

A create can replace that default. `placement` runs later and, when `regions` is present, overwrites the client list:

```go
client, err := sandbox.NewClient(modal.New(), &sandbox.Config{
    Region: sandbox.Value("us-east"),
})

// this sandbox: us-east, from the client
client.Create(ctx, &sandbox.CreateOptions{
    Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Image{Image: &sandbox.ImageSource{Reference: "alpine:3.21"}}},
})

// this sandbox: eu-west, and also a cloud
client.Create(ctx, &sandbox.CreateOptions{
    Source: &sandbox.SandboxSource{Kind: &sandbox.Image{Image: &sandbox.ImageSource{Reference: "alpine:3.21"}}},
    Placement: &sandbox.Placement{
        Cloud:   sandbox.Value("aws"),
        Regions: []string{"eu-west"},
    },
})
```

`placement.cloud` has no client-level equivalent. Modal is the provider that accepts it (`aws`, `gcp`, `oci`, `auto`). An empty `regions` list is also a real value: the test keeps it as an empty slice, so the client default `"us-east"` is not put back.

`SandboxInfo.region` is a third field. It is the location the provider reported after creation, not the preference you sent.

Modal already follows that rule. Setting `Config.region` for a Modal client succeeds. It becomes the region for every create that does not set its own.

```ts
await using client = new Client({
  provider: modal(),
  region: "us-east",
  // ...
});

// uses us-east
await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } },
});

// this request uses eu-west
await client.create({
  source: { image: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } },
  placement: { regions: ["eu-west"] },
});
```

The client copies `region` into the create first. `mapPlacement` runs after that and, when `placement.regions` is present, replaces the list:

```94:95:sdks/typescript/providers/modal/src/mappings/mappings.ts
  if (config.region !== undefined)
    params.regions = [nonblankConfig(config.region, "region")];
```

```182:183:sdks/typescript/providers/modal/src/mappings/mappings.ts
  if (v.cloud !== undefined) p.cloud = v.cloud;
  if (v.regions !== undefined) p.regions = [...v.regions];
```

An empty `regions: []` is a real override too. Modal keeps that empty list and does not put `us-east` back.

Daytona is the provider that rejects the per-request value. `Config.region` is accepted and stored as the client target, so every create uses it. Any `placement` on that create stops in Kit:

```ts
await client.create({
  source: { snapshot: { reference: "daytona-small" } },
  placement: { regions: ["eu"] },
});
// ErrorKind.Unsupported, field "placement"
// "per-request Daytona placement is unsupported; use Config.region"
```

Daytona's own SDK can take one target on a single create and have it override the client target. Kit does not map that. `placement` also carries `cloud` and a list of regions, and Daytona has neither of those on a create.

So the rule you described is already Modal's rule. Extending it to every provider means mapping one Daytona region from `placement.regions` when that list has a single entry, and still rejecting `placement.cloud`, more than one region, and `placement.target`.

